Subclinical Hypogonadism
Undifferentiated or Subclinical Symptom Clusters
Borderline or low-normal total serum testosterone | Elevated LH/FSH (in primary cases) | Absence of severe classical hypogonadal symptoms

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: USC-024
Reason for Visit: Acute nocturnal distress and anxiety related to persistent, unexplained exhaustion, compounding minor incidental chest pain, requiring urgent evaluation to exclude an acute cardiopulmonary event.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated an urgent assessment was required to evaluate vague, persistent fatigue compounded by new sharp chest pain. Immediate clinical examination was mandated to rule out acute pericarditis, pleurisy, or atypical coronary syndrome, preventing severe cardiovascular deterioration prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I am so exhausted all the time that I can barely function, and tonight my chest started aching sharply when I breathe in, and I just can't take it anymore.
History of Present Illness: The patient presents with profound, vague persistent fatigue, a depressed mood, and a lack of drive. Upon detailed history, the patient admits to a mild decrease in libido, subtle reduction in spontaneous morning erections, and a mild decrease in muscle mass or strength over the last year. Tonight, the patient's anxiety peaked due to the onset of a new, sharp localized chest pain that exhibits inspiratory pain exacerbation.
Functional Status: Independent but struggling with motivation and energy.
Social/Safety Concerns: High somatic anxiety.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 126/80 mmHg | HR: 76 bpm | Temp: 36.8 C | RR: 16 /min | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 94 Kg
General: Patient appears chronically fatigued, mildly overweight, and anxious about the chest pain.
Physical Exam: Musculoskeletal and respiratory examination reveals the cause of the chest pain to be localized costochondritis, characterized by sharp localized chest pain and inspiratory pain exacerbation that is fully reproducible on sternal palpation. A previous outpatient review confirms a clear chest radiograph. General physical examination notes a mild increase in central adiposity. Urogenital exam (offered and accepted) reveals normal testicular volume on palpation without masses, and an absence of severe gynecomastia. Cranial nerve exam reveals normal visual fields without signs of a pituitary macroadenoma.
Environmental Observations: Safe, quiet home environment.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. No chronic medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Subclinical Hypogonadism - Status: Chronic underlying fatigue state with an acute, unrelated costochondritis flare.
Differentials:

Acute Pericarditis - Ruled out definitively due to the completely normal cardiac auscultation (no friction rub), lack of positional pain relief (leaning forward), and pain perfectly reproduced by chest wall palpation.

Pulmonary Embolism - Ruled out due to normal oxygen saturations, lack of resting tachycardia, and clear lung fields.

Major Depressive Disorder - Ruled out as the sole primary cause due to the specific clustering of physiological endocrine symptoms (loss of morning erections, decreased muscle mass, central adiposity), though mood is secondarily affected.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low.

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient mandated an immediate after-hours home assessment due to severe acute distress driven by chronic, profound exhaustion culminating in sharp, pleuritic chest pain. Telephone triage appropriately identified the urgent need to exclude acute pericarditis or evolving pulmonary embolism. Rigorous clinical palpation and auscultation definitively isolated the chest pain to superficial costochondral inflammation, completely ruling out deep cardiopulmonary pathology. By identifying the true mechanical nature of the acute pain and contextualizing it within a chronic, subclinical endocrine deficiency, the clinician completely arrested the patient's panic cycle and averted a highly unnecessary emergency department admission.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered oral NSAIDs for the costochondritis. The expected investigation results include borderline low or low-normal total serum testosterone with normal luteinizing hormone levels, which will assist in confirming the underlying hypogonadal diagnosis via the regular GP.
Positive Behavioral Change: To manage the chest wall pain and improve sleep, apply a warm compress directly to the sternum for 20 minutes before bed; physically relaxing the intercostal muscles will reduce the inspiratory pain and lower your acute anxiety.
Home-Based Services: None indicated.
DME/Safety: None required.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in one week for a comprehensive morning hormone panel (Testosterone, LH, FSH, Prolactin).

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate transfer to the Emergency Department or call 000 if the chest pain suddenly becomes crushing, radiates to the jaw or left arm, or if severe shortness of breath develops at rest.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive; accurate diagnosis requires fasting morning blood tests.
Clinical Course: The expected natural course involves chronic, stable fatigue and low mood unless formal endocrinological intervention and significant lifestyle/weight modifications are achieved.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate emergency attention if your chest pain changes from a sharp ache when breathing to a heavy, crushing sensation in the center of your chest.

